[info] >  PitchLab: Raw request data: {"idea":"Skymart is a mixed-use development model that builds supermarkets with residential apartments above, including potential airspace developments over railway corridors. The goal is to maximise underutilised urban land while creating a scalable national supermarket network. The long-term vision is to establish Skymart as a third major supermarket chain across New Zealand.\n","category":"Marketplace","problem":"New Zealand faces a housing shortage and inefficient land use in urban areas, particularly around transport hubs. At the same time, the supermarket sector is dominated by a duopoly, limiting competition and innovation. There is a lack of integrated developments that combine retail convenience with high-density living.\n","targetMarket":"The target market includes urban renters, commuters, and working professionals, particularly in high-demand areas like Auckland. The supermarket component targets everyday consumers looking for convenience and competitive pricing. The broader opportunity spans residential property demand and the multi-billion-dollar grocery sector.\n","experience":"The founder has strong financial, commercial, and project evaluation experience, with a background in accounting, consulting, and infrastructure-related ventures. There is proven capability in structuring deals, analysing feasibility, and engaging stakeholders. However, large-scale property development execution and supermarket operations experience will need to be supplemented.\n","capacity":"Current capacity is limited, with a small team and competing commitments across multiple ventures. Execution of a project at this scale will require external partners, including developers, contractors, and operators. Without scaling the team, delivery risk is high.\n","budget":"An MVP could be a single pilot site development, estimated at $10M–$20M depending on complexity (especially over-rail builds). A lower-cost MVP alternative is securing land/air rights and producing a fully consented, investor-ready project. Early validation can be achieved with <$100K through feasibility studies, concept design, and stakeholder engagement.\n","network":"The founder has access to networks in construction, transport, and Pacific infrastructure, along with some exposure to investors and government stakeholders. However, stronger connections are needed with institutional investors, supermarket operators, and large-scale developers. Building these relationships will be critical to unlocking capital and execution capability.\n"} {"user":"PitchLab: Raw request data: {\"idea\":\"Skymart is a mixed-use development model that builds supermarkets with residential apartments above, including potential airspace developments over railway corridors. The goal is to maximise underutilised urban land while creating a scalable national supermarket network. The long-term vision is to establish Skymart as a third major supermarket chain across New Zealand.\\n\",\"category\":\"Marketplace\",\"problem\":\"New Zealand faces a housing shortage and inefficient land use in urban areas, particularly around transport hubs. At the same time, the supermarket sector is dominated by a duopoly, limiting competition and innovation. There is a lack of integrated developments that combine retail convenience with high-density living.\\n\",\"targetMarket\":\"The target market includes urban renters, commuters, and working professionals, particularly in high-demand areas like Auckland. The supermarket component targets everyday consumers looking for convenience and competitive pricing. The broader opportunity spans residential property demand and the multi-billion-dollar grocery sector.\\n\",\"experience\":\"The founder has strong financial, commercial, and project evaluation experience, with a background in accounting, consulting, and infrastructure-related ventures. There is proven capability in structuring deals, analysing feasibility, and engaging stakeholders. However, large-scale property development execution and supermarket operations experience will need to be supplemented.\\n\",\"capacity\":\"Current capacity is limited, with a small team and competing commitments across multiple ventures. Execution of a project at this scale will require external partners, including developers, contractors, and operators. Without scaling the team, delivery risk is high.\\n\",\"budget\":\"An MVP could be a single pilot site development, estimated at $10M–$20M depending on complexity (especially over-rail builds). A lower-cost MVP alternative is securing land/air rights and producing a fully consented, investor-ready project. Early validation can be achieved with <$100K through feasibility studies, concept design, and stakeholder engagement.\\n\",\"network\":\"The founder has access to networks in construction, transport, and Pacific infrastructure, along with some exposure to investors and government stakeholders. However, stronger connections are needed with institutional investors, supermarket operators, and large-scale developers. Building these relationships will be critical to unlocking capital and execution capability.\\n\"}","metadata":{"emulator":{"name":"functions"},"function":{"name":"us-central1-pitchLabEvaluation"},"extension":{},"message":"\u001b[90m> \u001b[39m PitchLab: Raw request data: {\"idea\":\"Skymart is a mixed-use development model that builds supermarkets with residential apartments above, including potential airspace developments over railway corridors. The goal is to maximise underutilised urban land while creating a scalable national supermarket network. The long-term vision is to establish Skymart as a third major supermarket chain across New Zealand.\\n\",\"category\":\"Marketplace\",\"problem\":\"New Zealand faces a housing shortage and inefficient land use in urban areas, particularly around transport hubs. At the same time, the supermarket sector is dominated by a duopoly, limiting competition and innovation. There is a lack of integrated developments that combine retail convenience with high-density living.\\n\",\"targetMarket\":\"The target market includes urban renters, commuters, and working professionals, particularly in high-demand areas like Auckland. The supermarket component targets everyday consumers looking for convenience and competitive pricing. The broader opportunity spans residential property demand and the multi-billion-dollar grocery sector.\\n\",\"experience\":\"The founder has strong financial, commercial, and project evaluation experience, with a background in accounting, consulting, and infrastructure-related ventures. There is proven capability in structuring deals, analysing feasibility, and engaging stakeholders. However, large-scale property development execution and supermarket operations experience will need to be supplemented.\\n\",\"capacity\":\"Current capacity is limited, with a small team and competing commitments across multiple ventures. Execution of a project at this scale will require external partners, including developers, contractors, and operators. Without scaling the team, delivery risk is high.\\n\",\"budget\":\"An MVP could be a single pilot site development, estimated at $10M–$20M depending on complexity (especially over-rail builds). A lower-cost MVP alternative is securing land/air rights and producing a fully consented, investor-ready project. Early validation can be achieved with <$100K through feasibility studies, concept design, and stakeholder engagement.\\n\",\"network\":\"The founder has access to networks in construction, transport, and Pacific infrastructure, along with some exposure to investors and government stakeholders. However, stronger connections are needed with institutional investors, supermarket operators, and large-scale developers. Building these relationships will be critical to unlocking capital and execution capability.\\n\"}"}}
